From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 11:05:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] engine: force a draw after feeding pty output

A ghostty pane stopped repainting partway through any long output. The
terminal was fine - scrolling revealed the whole listing, correctly
parsed - but the screen stayed stale until something unrelated woke it.
Output did not repaint; neither did resizing; scrolling did.

libghostty schedules repaints through its `wakeup` async, which
coalesces by design - the comment on the neighbouring `draw_now` async
says so: "This does not coalesce like the wakeup does." A notify that
lands after the renderer has already sampled the terminal state is folded
into a wake that has finished its work, so the last batch never reaches
the screen.

Measured in one run: queueRender notified 80 times, processOutput ran 80
times, the renderer thread woke fewer than 20 times, and drawFrame
stopped being called entirely while bytes kept arriving. Ruled out along
the way, each with evidence rather than argument: lost bytes (58 KB
parsed during the freeze), a skipped back-buffer copy (never happened),
deadlock (every thread idle, children at a prompt), and
must_draw_from_app_thread (not declared for this apprt).

ghostty_surface_draw is drawFrame(sync = true), which skips the
needs_redraw gate and is documented as safe from another thread. It is
exactly what hwnd-host does on WM_PAINT - which is why the harness never
showed this in three phases of work, and why a XAML pane, having no
paint pump, showed it immediately. Composition mode was correlated, not
causal.

Verified: `dir C:\Windows\System32` now renders all 4796 entries and ends
at the prompt with no scrolling or resizing.

This is arguably compensating for an upstream shortcoming - output ought
to drive its own repaint without the embedder forcing frames, and any
embedder not pumping paint messages would hit this. Worth raising
upstream; the retro should decide whether we carry a patch that makes the
tail of the wakeup non-coalescing instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ee27Fih3vSPYsqrUBpUoc
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 20 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index d37aa06d7..befe27c1e 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -310,6 +310,26 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         ghostty_surface_write_pty_output(_surface,
                                          reinterpret_cast<const uint8_t*>(_utf8Scratch.data()),
                                          _utf8Scratch.size());
+
+        // Then force the frame out.
+        //
+        // Parsing schedules a render through libghostty's `wakeup` async,
+        // which *coalesces by design* (its own comment contrasts it with the
+        // non-coalescing `draw_now`). A notify that lands after the renderer
+        // has already sampled the terminal state is folded into a wake that
+        // has done its work, so the final batch never reaches the screen: the
+        // pane freezes mid-output and stays frozen until something unrelated
+        // wakes it, which is exactly what scrolling did.
+        //
+        // Measured: 80 notifies produced fewer than 20 wakes, and drawFrame
+        // stopped being called entirely while the parser kept running.
+        //
+        // ghostty_surface_draw is drawFrame(sync = true), which skips the
+        // needs_redraw gate and is documented as safe to call from another
+        // thread. It is what hwnd-host does on WM_PAINT - which is why the
+        // harness never showed this and a XAML pane, having no paint pump,
+        // always did.
+        ghostty_surface_draw(_surface);
     }
 
     void GhosttyControlCore::_connectionStateChangedHandler()
